  "summary": "Today's health-tech landscape is shaped by several critical developments. The FDA and regulators are moving quickly on emerging therapies—approving GSK's novel hepatitis B drug and accelerating personalized gene therapy pathways—while also grappling with oversight of generative AI tools that increasingly perform clinical functions. The sector is consolidating strategically, with Willow expanding maternal health via two acquisitions, Alcon investing $550 million in neurotech cell therapy, and Gilead paying $1.3 billion for an oral peptide to rival J&J's immunology drug. Meanwhile, operational pressures are mounting across healthcare: automation startups like ClarityCare are raising capital to meet new federal prior authorization deadlines, remote monitoring programs are expanding for chronic diseases, and rural hospitals continue lagging in AI adoption despite clear clinical benefits. Safety concerns persist in emerging categories—Artiva is shutting down after deaths in an autoimmune cell therapy trial, signaling that translating cancer therapy approaches to autoimmune disease remains risky. Broader policy questions are also surfacing, from state attorneys general challenging pharmaceutical data demands under 340B programs to the AMA calling for risk-based GenAI oversight and concerns about Medicare's approach to modernizing maternity care.",
